og": [{"timestamp": "2026-06-22T10:29:07Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#27946198313", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "United states organization of psychiatrists Headquartered in Washington, D.C.. Founded 1844.", "newValue": "The American Psychiatric Association (APA), founded in 1844, is a pivotal organization in the field of psychiatry, headquartered in Washington, D.C. It emerged during a transformative period in the United States when mental health began to gain recognition as a legitimate field of medical practice. The APA played a crucial role in establishing psychiatric standards and practices, solidifying psychiatry's place within the broader medical community and shaping the treatment of mental illness across North America.

The APA's founding was catalyzed by the growing awareness of mental health issues and the inadequacies of existing treatment paradigms, which often relied on harsh methods. With influential figures such as Dr. Benjamin Rush, often referred to as the father of American psychiatry, the organization focused on promoting humane treatment for individuals with mental disorders. It was instrumental in creating the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders (DSM), first published in 1952, which standardized psychiatric diagnoses and treatment protocols, influencing psychiatric practice worldwide.

